  "about": {
    "summary": "What trains what",
    "p": "Absolute pitch barely transfers to playing. The skills that do are functional: hearing a note as a scale degree inside a key, hearing a chord's quality, and hearing where a progression is going. Chapters C and D are the ones that show up when you improvise.",
    "li": [
      "Degrees before intervals. Once <span class=\"hint\">4</span> sounds like <span class=\"hint\">4</span> rather than \"a fourth above something\", you can find a melody by ear in real time.",
      "Sing the answer before you click it. Passive listening plateaus fast.",
      "Do the chord chapters with arpeggio off. Block chords force you to hear the whole stack, not the top line.",
      "Jam tab is the transfer test: loop a progression, improvise, and check yourself against the highlighted chord tones.",
      "Ten focused minutes daily beats an hour once a week."
    ]
  },
